---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
const entries = [
  { v: '1.1 of Thermodynamic Bounds and 0.2 of Human Language Encoding at Machine Speed (site)', date: '28 October 2026', items: [
    'Both papers name the paper, or use the passive, where they narrated an author or used a vague "we"; no claim of either paper changes. The eight translations of the first (Spanish, French, German, Portuguese, Japanese, Simplified Chinese, Hindi and Arabic) are corrected in the same places. The about page, the catalog summary, the paper page description and the data page of the second state the energy attempt that the paper records, and the paper and the catalog summary spell out the format names at their first use.',
    'The PDFs of both papers, and the LaTeX PDF of the first, are to be regenerated from this text.',
  ]},
  { v: '1.24 of The Reality Code Engine', date: '28 October 2026', items: [
    'The abstract, Sections 1, 2, 3.6, 3.7, 4.6, 5.1, 6, 11, 12.5 and 13, the about page and the catalog summary state lifting every language into the Form and emitting every language from it as a design of the Reality Code Engine (RCE): the proposal spec/proposals/rce-form.md of openIE-dev/rce, with its acceptance levels 0 to 6. They separate it from what exists: four emitters that translate the checked syntax tree of a program, not the Form, and one public lift probe for a Go pattern.',
    'Sections 2.1 and 2.2 state the gears, the gate and promotion as the design of the engine. Section 3.6 states the acceptance levels 0 and 4 of rce-form.md, the three proposals it builds on and its five node kinds as the proposal defines them, and the plain-words list and the about page say that the tools that would convert other languages are not built; Section 3.6 names the pinned oracles and recorded corpora for Go, Python and JavaScript under lang/ of openIE-dev/rce, which a lift would be judged against, and states level 4 with its rule that a case kept for a resource limit blocks it. Section 4.5 names the 520 conformance tests that do not apply to an emitter. Section 4.6 says which of its boundary checks the public tools make (the log chain on replay, and the emitted Rust, Python and JavaScript programs against the reference) and that the others are design. Section 5.1 states what the emitter tests compare: the program output with the reference evaluator\'s observations, and on the conformance suite the outputs and the hash of the last log entry; the whole reference trace, byte for byte, is the gate the emit registry proposal sets. Section 5.1 and the theorem table state that the tests of the order-preserving pass before the emitters (theorem T34) run on Rust, Python and JavaScript, not on every target; the theorem table expands A-normal form (ANF) at its first use. Section 6 states the gate of the L0 and L1 repair rungs as identity with whitespace removed, which is what the scripts compare. Section 12.5 states that "use" is the only way to bring a library module in since openIE-dev/rce 5e701e33, and that the replay of the fabric contract is proved on a model of the host (theorem T37), with the example logs re-folded through the reference checker, while the replay of the reference executor is tested against its live run (tests/host_runtime.rs).',
    'Section 4.5 gives the conformance standing of the emitters at openIE-dev/rce 464bd98b: Rust, Python and JavaScript pass 1,459 tests each with 0 failures; Scala passes 52, fails 1,189 and has 591 unsupported of 1,832 results. The thesis and the conclusion cite that count instead of saying that the output prints the reference trace. Section 10 gains two items: the Scala emitter brought to agreement, and the design of rce-form.md built.',
    'Section 1 states that RCE has no measured energy figure and that every est_j is a model; Section 6.3 labels the ladder estimate as one.',
    'Section 10, item 10, and Section 12.1 agree on how the Lean model is tied to the checker: extraction from Lean in Phase 2, not a refinement proof to the Rust interpreter. Section 12.2 states that effect handlers are a non-goal of draft 0.9 (F.2) and gives the argument of the record for the exclusion: ownership and the totality bound rather than replay, with handlers required to be one-shot, and one-shot, tail-resumptive handlers as the fragment the Form could admit.',
    'Citations in Section 12 are renumbered to match the reference list: from [158] on, the in-text numbers pointed one or two entries past their sources. Reference [174] is not cited.',
    'flowG, the parent project of RCE, is named in the section on the universality thesis. Uses of "fit" for a stored solution are replaced, in the text, in the design-space figure and on the opportunity-map boards; the term defined in Definition 2 is unchanged.',
    'Figure 1, panel 6, states the projection of every surface and the choice of the cheapest form by estimated joules as a design (Rust, Python and JavaScript pass the conformance suite; Scala does not yet), and its caption points to the standing of the emitters. The about page caption says which parts of the overview figure are design: the emit targets drawn out of the Form, and the row of compute fabrics, since the reference executor runs on CPUs only. The opportunity-map page states that its rebuild column is the design of RCE, and its second board states the projection of every language as a design.',
    'RCE-JEPA: its first falsifier (RJ-F1) is stated for the languages whose lift is built and defeats the claim that all code can be brought into the Form by lift; the symbols of the Form (δ, D, λ) match the RCE paper; the thesis section and its chain table mark the published first runs of H1, H7 and H8 as measured at the software layer and keep their other arms planned; the architecture figure no longer places Scala under T9-L. The chain table and the status section state that all five invariants of the H7 run were checked on the 398 mutants with a trace, and determinism and re-acceptance on the other 877, and the code paths of the three runs name the experiments repository; the transition function is computed by the reference evaluator, with its determinism and totality proved on a core calculus; the emitters are described by their conformance counts. The game-engines study states the Form as the RCE paper does, defines the outputs of a step and gives Scala\'s standing.',
    'The data page gives both runs of the H7 fuzzed arm, and the download page says that the public checker does not yet refuse a malformed event at entry.',
    'The specification notebook at /rce/spec/ is regenerated from openIE-dev/rce 2364aa97 and shows draft 0.9, which the paper names as the current draft; its index gives the repository counts at that commit and keeps those at e416fd0 that the paper quotes.',
    'The PNG copies of the first two figures of the RCE paper, of the RCE-JEPA architecture figure and of the three opportunity-map boards are regenerated from this version; the rebuild column of the third board no longer describes the design with "fit". The PDFs of the RCE paper, RCE-JEPA and the game-engines study are to be regenerated from this version.',
  ]},
  { v: '1.23 of The Reality Code Engine', date: '27 October 2026', items: [
    'Results that only the engine produced are withdrawn until each is rebuilt on public code; Section 10 lists them with their rebuild tracks. Figures whose content was those results are no longer embedded, and the remaining figures are renumbered 1 to 6.',
    'Section 5.5: the model-proposer run is reported from its public record (openie-research-experiments, rce/bench/results/llm-proposer-form-demo.form.json): 18 proposals, 13 distinct; 15 refused, 12 distinct; the 3 admitted are one rewrite, (pipe pass ?a) => ?a, under three names, which repeats a rule the engine already had.',
    'The compiled engine demo at /demos/rce-engine/ and the redirect at /playground/rce/ are removed. The playground is the reference checker in the browser at openie-dev.github.io/rce, built from public code.',
    'The LaTeX PDF and its source are removed until they are regenerated from this version.',
    'The about page shows the overview figure of openIE-dev/rce (emit targets Rust, Python, JavaScript and Scala) and no longer shows the status figure of Haskell, Julia and editor counts, which that repository removed on 16 October 2026 (78511532) because no public code reproduces them.',
    'The translations of this paper are marked stale and show the translated title and deck (the one-line summary) until they are redone against version 1.23.',
  ]},
  { v: '1.4 of The Reality Code Engine (site)', date: '14 October 2026', items: [
    'One RCE playground: the reference checker in the browser at openie-dev.github.io/rce is the RCE playground.',
    'The paper, its translations, the LaTeX PDF, the home page, llms.txt and the WebMCP tools link the playground by this name.',
  ]},
  { v: '1.4 of The Reality Code Engine', date: '13 October 2026', items: [
    'Section 3.7: specification 2.7 now states the per-call-site cost rule of C.15 (openIE-dev/rce ac94a8f); /rce/spec/ regenerated.',
    'Section 7: Software Heritage and Rosetta Code cited as [129] to [131]; archive counters reread (30.3 billion file contents, 448 million origins).',
    'Nine figures added (Figures 5 to 10, 12, 13 and 15); earlier Figures 5 and 6 renumbered 11 and 14. Translations, including German, remain at version 1.1.',
    'Site: new Opportunity map page with three boards and their sources.',
  ]},
  { v: '1.1', date: '11 October 2026', items: [
    'All papers: version and revision date under the title.',
    'The Reality Code Engine: rule-authoring timeline against the corpus freeze (corpus20 is a development set); n and a 95% interval on the headline result; gross beside idle-subtracted RAPL joules; host described as an x86 laptop; Section 5.1 renamed to differential testing; scope of the ASM theorem stated, with the Form as a modelling choice; repair, overfitting and benchmark-bias lineage; held-out and baseline protocols (8.4, 8.5); Falsifiers, Work to finish and Conclusion sections.',
    'The Promised Trillion Dollar AI-Coding Market: McKinsey, Goldman Sachs, GitHub, Gartner, Sequoia and Stargate rows; productivity evidence table; Microsoft pre-tax and net figures; Cursor repricing; the 9 to 900 times price range, LLMflation and the DeepSeek cut; bundling by the platform owner; the price elasticity; coverage proposition E5; conditional conclusion.',
    'Thermodynamic Bounds (agency): the thesis read through its gate, against Burnell, Zhou, Laber and existing gated metrics; objections to the floor (Section 3.0); Koyuk and Seifert for driven acts; Jordan on Margolus-Levitin; the estimator replacement; falsifier outcomes in Section 5.2; the Sudoku grid stated as inside the agent wall.',
    'Metered Commit Gates: KV260 figures relabeled SOM-level with the rail cited, seed-fabric result marked inconclusive; runtime-enforcement lineage; cascade lineage; II.3 renamed cost model.',
    'Task Satiation: sections renumbered (old 2.8 to 2.10 and 3.6 to 3.8 are now 3.6 to 3.11, with the old anchors kept); rebound canon; predicate endogeneity; H1 from public trajectories; tokens and joules; identification limits.',
    'Spell Check is Global: title tidied; the few stated as a price class; connected reach from ITU; premium tier moved up a layer; bundling economics; grammatical error correction and the energy trade-off.',
    'Site: navigation and footer list every paper and PDF; one measured_j definition; nine glossary terms; one Notational Intelligence definition; About count; catalog hero wording.',
  ]},
];
---
<Layout title="Changelog | OpenIE Research" description="Dated revisions of the OpenIE research papers.">
  <main class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-16">
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl text-brand-white">Changelog</h1>
    {entries.map((e) => (
      <section class="mt-8">
        <h2 class="font-display text-xl text-brand-white">Version {e.v}, {e.date}</h2>
        <ul class="mt-3 list-disc pl-6 font-body text-sm text-brand-slate leading-relaxed">
          {e.items.map((i) => <li class="mt-2">{i}</li>)}
        </ul>
      </section>
    ))}
  </main>
</Layout>
